    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town was named for John J. Dollard, a young schoolteacher and doctor who served as postmaster after the post office opened in 1877.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/dallardsville-tx"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1888 the Dallardsville school was organized.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/dallardsville-tx"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "By 1890 the town had seventy-five residents, three churches, and a public school; cotton and wool were the primary shipments from area farmers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/dallardsville-tx"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Subsequent discoveries of oil and natural gas at the Segno fields to the south led to new growth.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/dallardsville-tx"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "In 1983 and 1984 additional oil and gas fields were found to the north and west of town at the Dallardsville fields.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
